<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/ea3a4c64-dac3-4660-809a-8ad5ced8997b> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2021-02-25T01:03:50"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "Theoretically, it is useful to consider the complete set of plants belonging to all species living in a particular place and time as plant community (community sensu lato see Vellend 2016). In practice, studies often focus on a subset of the whole plant community chosen on the basis of growth form, taxonomy or particular interactions (Morin 2011)." ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-19T05:29:51+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "Morin, P. J. Community ecology. Wiley. (2011).",
        "Vellend, Mark. The theory of ecological communities (MPB-57). Princeton University Press, 2020." ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/881bcb98-1c31-4710-a9a4-58dbe1bc4a95> ;
    skos:definition "Group of plants representing multiple species living in a specified place and time." ;
    skos:example "1. Trees, shrubs, and grasses in an area.; 2. Above ground biomass of all woody species in a specific area." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:notation "plant-community" ;
    skos:prefLabel "plant community"@en ;
    schema:citation "Morin, P. J. Community ecology. Wiley. (2011).",
        "Vellend, Mark. The theory of ecological communities (MPB-57). Princeton University Press, 2020." .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/ea785694-dd98-485b-b504-6b3c81674792> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2024-04-16T04:16:45"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:modified "2025-10-14T03:05:33"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "Australian Bureau of Statistics. (Jul2021-Jun2026). Australia and State/Territory. ABS. https://www.abs.gov.au/statistics/standards/australian-statistical-geography-standard-asgs-edition-3/jul2021-jun2026/main-structure-and-greater-capital-city-statistical-areas/australia-and-stateterritory." ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/05dac53a-269c-4699-9673-bf99a9406b14> ;
    skos:definition "Australia is broken up into the States and Territories that are recognised in the Australian Statistical Geography Standard (ASGS) as: New South Wales, Victoria, Queensland, South Australia, Western Australia, Tasmania, Northern Territory, Australian Capital Territory and Other Territories (Jervis Bay Territory, Territory of Christmas Island, Territory of the Cocos (Keeling) Islands and Norfolk Island)"@en ;
    skos:example "Any ecological observations made/collected within a particular state or Territory. For example Biodiversity metrics (Shannon or Simpson Index) for the Queensland State.",
        "Australian States and Territories serve as a feature of interest for reporting biodiversity, habitat, and ecosystem monitoring datasets at the State and Territory level. Each government represents a relatively large adminstrative unit of land and/or water containing assemblage of natural communities managed within the government administrative area." ;
    skos:historyNote "Added as a narrower of 'administrative area' in the TERN Feature Type (Release version, 2024)." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "States and Territories"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/ecb855ed-50e1-4299-8491-861759ef40b7> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2021-04-21T06:30:18"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "An animal indvidual is a physiological independent individual that has some kind of autonomous agency and has a life cycle that allow them to form some kind of reproductive lineages. Operationally, in some cases superorganisms or colonies (e.g. corals) may be considered a special kind of individual." ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-19T05:29:51+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "Wilson, Robert A., and Matthew Barker. The biological notion of individual. (2007)." ;
    skos:broadMatch <http://linkeddata.ge.imati.cnr.it/resource/EARTh/45460>,
        <http://www.eionet.europa.eu/gemet/concept/5914> ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/f6a6cdf8-d8cf-4e2d-b2c1-febd8a41a07b> ;
    skos:definition "Physiologically distinct and independent organism." ;
    skos:example "All animal individuals should be identified as a unique individual based on some sort of unique ID and any observations or measurements on that individual animal is unique to that individual. For instance, a Wombat is tagged with a unique identifier in a study area for studying its feeding and sleeping behaviour over time." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:notation "animal-individual" ;
    skos:prefLabel "animal individual"@en ;
    schema:citation "Wilson, Robert A., and Matthew Barker. The biological notion of individual. (2007)." .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/edebcd9a-a42e-49ba-a629-056cb1a7ba73> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2022-09-19T23:25:46"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "Dead or alive plant organic matter characteristics relevant to its flammability, sampled in a define area." ;
    dcterms:modified "2023-06-23T05:55:59"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "https://www.ecologyandsociety.org/vol7/iss1/art9/des_fuel_load.html" ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/df7fd606-8145-4206-b66e-b86444888802> ;
    skos:definition "Dead or alive plant organic matter characteristics relevant to its flammability, sampled in a define area." ;
    skos:example "The fuel load of the forest stand." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:notation "stand-vegetation-fuel" ;
    skos:prefLabel "stand vegetation fuel"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/f0a62303-5d37-47eb-a940-58661698cdd7> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2024-04-18T03:47:26"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "These are plants such as lichens or mosses that do not have vascular tubes/tissues to transport food and water. It belongs to the division Bryophyta, which includes liverwort, and moss." ;
    dcterms:modified "2024-04-21T22:55:11"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/ea6c4b26-2fa1-4d0e-9413-b53f248f2269> ;
    skos:definition "A non-vascular plant population refers to the total count of individuals of a particular species of non-vascular plants." ;
    skos:example "A count of the total number of Usnea scabrida (lichen species) sampled from a rocky area." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "non-vascular plant population"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/f31c7f93-78d1-47de-92c2-c5541aa5f8e8> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2024-04-18T03:50:07"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "Woody plants are perennials that produce secondary growth in the form of wood. They include trees, palms, shrubs and cycads." ;
    dcterms:modified "2024-04-18T04:47:17"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/95e72362-8575-46e9-bc60-a45f48b95390> ;
    skos:definition "The group of indivdiuals of a particular species of woody vascular plant at a specific region and time." ;
    skos:example "The count of Fan Palm (Licuala ramsayi) in a 1 hectare plot in the Daintree National Park sampled post-cyclone in 2023." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "woody vascular plant population"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/f65eb204-4a58-485f-b339-4396a05ce251> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2024-04-16T04:31:55"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "Australia has thousands of national parks across the State and Territory agencies, of which six Major Parks are managed by the Commonwealth Government."@en ;
    dcterms:modified "2024-06-28T01:05:12"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "https://www.dcceew.gov.au/parks-heritage/national-parks" ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/05dac53a-269c-4699-9673-bf99a9406b14> ;
    skos:definition "A list of National Parks within Australia that are managed by the Australian, State and Territory governments." ;
    skos:example "Some popular national parks include: 1) Kakadu National Park; 2) Booderee National Park; 3) Uluru - Kata Tjuta National Park; 4) Great Barrier Reef Marine Park; 5) Norfolk, Christmas and Cocos (Keeling) Islands"@en ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "National Parks"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/f6e064c6-e2e9-4f9a-90dd-66434a93d985> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2022-04-20T04:18:46"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "A population of ants (insects from the family Formicidae) is a collection of individuals belonging to the same ant species, living in the same area." ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-19T05:29:51+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "Begon, Michael, Martin Mortimer, and David J. Thompson. Population ecology: a unified study of animals and plants. John Wiley & Sons, 2009." ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/c892f830-4c65-4ac2-a81a-323e2ef42657> ;
    skos:definition "Group of individuals of the same ant species (family Formicidae) living within a given area." ;
    skos:example "Yellow crazy ant population detected in north Queensland." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:notation "ant-population" ;
    skos:prefLabel "ant population"@en ;
    schema:citation "Begon, Michael, Martin Mortimer, and David J. Thompson. Population ecology: a unified study of animals and plants. John Wiley & Sons, 2009." .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/f7b324fe-c042-463f-ae93-11ba2efcf0bb> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2024-04-18T01:29:14"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "Any individual woody vascular plant, i.e., belonging to a shrub, tree, palm or cycad. They have vascular tissues consisting of xylem, which carries water and nutrients from the roots up the stem to the leaves, and the phloem, which carries sugar produced in the leaves to other parts of the plant." ;
    dcterms:modified "2024-04-18T03:10:57"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/57c778e4-5a82-4dfd-8087-d616ac629ab1> ;
    skos:definition "A woody vascular plant individual is an individual perennial plant that produce secondary growth in the form of wood." ;
    skos:example "An individual tree or shrub, tagged and identified to its species level and studied for its diameter growth over multiple site visits." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "woody vascular plant individual"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/ffbe3c8c-23f1-4fc4-8aaf-dfba9f12576c> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-02-24T22:37:21"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/5bf7ae21-a454-440b-bdd7-f2fe982d8de4> ;
    skos:definition "Parent site." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "parent site"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/1a017998-f59d-43f8-ac47-bea75e50d24d> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2024-04-17T06:37:18"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:modified "2025-10-14T03:09:48"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "Global Biodiversity Information Facility (GBIF)" ;
    skos:altLabel "fungal organisms" ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/3d355008-117e-48ec-a822-adb09495ac78> ;
    skos:definition "A biological kingdom comprising eukaryotic organisms such as mushrooms, yeasts, and moulds, characterized by chitinous cell walls and heterotrophic nutrition through the absorption of dissolved organic matter." ;
    skos:example "Mycorrhizal fungal abundance in Eucalyptus woodland soils measured via ITS DNA sequencing." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "fungi"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Fungi serve as a feature of interest for biodiversity, soil, and ecosystem function studies. Observations may include fungal biomass, species richness, community composition, mycorrhizal colonization, or DNA-based taxa counts." .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/2e122e23-881c-43fa-a921-a8745f016ceb> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:description "Plant specimens usually are dried and mounted on a sheet of paper, but they may also be stored in boxes or kept in liquid preservatives. Plant specimens may be used for identification purposes, morphometrics, plant taxonomy and nomenclature, and the study of geographic distribution of plants." ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-19T05:29:51+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "Heberling, J. Mason, L. Alan Prather, and Stephen J. Tonsor. The changing uses of herbarium data in an era of global change: An overview using automated content analysis. BioScience 69.10 (2019): 812-822." ;
    skos:altLabel "plant voucher specimen" ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/881bcb98-1c31-4710-a9a4-58dbe1bc4a95> ;
    skos:closeMatch <http://rs.tdwg.org/dwc/terms/PreservedSpecimen> ;
    skos:definition "A plant specimen is a part of an individual plant or whole plant preserved, usually in a herbarium." ;
    skos:example "A vouchered sample of an individual plant preserved in a herbarium sheet." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:notation "plant-specimen" ;
    skos:prefLabel "plant specimen" ;
    schema:citation "Heberling, J. Mason, L. Alan Prather, and Stephen J. Tonsor. The changing uses of herbarium data in an era of global change: An overview using automated content analysis. BioScience 69.10 (2019): 812-822." .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/32834f36-a478-45be-97f4-ff2ff51e9f5c> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2021-03-15T02:16:26"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "A stratum is an easily seen layer of foliage and branches of a distinct and measurable height. One or more strata can exist in a patch of vegetation." ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-19T05:29:51+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "Hnatiuk, R.J., Thackway, R., and Walker, J. Vegetation. In National Committee on Soil and Terrain (2009) ‘Australian soil and land survey field handbook (3rd edn).’ (CSIRO Publishing: Melbourne).",
        "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ENVO_01000355" ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/2be2d5ce-ca9d-4dff-8e79-948983944f95> ;
    skos:definition "A layer which is determined by a form of vegetation and is of a distinct height class in the vegetation." ;
    skos:example "The uppemost part of a rainforest canopy; an understory layer comprised of shrubs." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:notation "vegetation-stratum" ;
    skos:prefLabel "vegetation stratum"@en ;
    schema:citation "Hnatiuk, R.J., Thackway, R., and Walker, J. Vegetation. In National Committee on Soil and Terrain (2009) ‘Australian soil and land survey field handbook (3rd edn).’ (CSIRO Publishing: Melbourne)." .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/4381b1b0-d432-4764-aec3-a26d2b8d85af> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2023-06-23T03:39:01"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "<p>Australia boasts a rich variety of ecosystems, shaped by its diverse climate, geology, and native species. Here are some of the key ecosystem types found across the continent:</p> <p>1. Desert and Xeric Shrublands: The vast central region of Australia is characterized by arid deserts and shrublands, adapted to extreme heat and limited water availability.</p> <p>2. Tropical and Subtropical Regions: A smaller area hosts tropical and subtropical broadleaf forests, remnants of ecosystems that once dominated Australia millions of years ago.</p> <p>3. Mediterranean Forests and Woodlands: These ecosystems thrive in regions with a Mediterranean climate, featuring dry summers and mild, wet winters. They include iconic eucalyptus forests.</p> <p>4. Temperate Regions: These encompass temperate forests, grasslands, and woodlands. Eucalypt-dominated forests and diverse grassy plains are common.</p> <p>5. Montane ecosystems and Tundra: At higher elevations, one will find the montane ecosystems, including alpine meadows and tundra."^^rdf:HTML ;
    dcterms:modified "2025-10-14T03:32:08"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "http://purl.dataone.org/odo/ECSO_00000309",
        "https://soe.dcceew.gov.au/biodiversity/environment/ecosystems-and-habitats" ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/237df7e1-2d92-4e9d-851e-28439bf5d8d8> ;
    skos:definition "Ecosystems are dynamic entities composed of the biological community and the abiotic environment. They are a system formed by the interaction of a community of organisms with their physical environment." ;
    skos:example "Ecosystem releated observations such as for reporting biodiversity, habitat, and ecosystem monitoring datasets at the Ecosystem level. For example, comparing carbon dioxide concentrations coming out of rainforest ecosystems and desert ecosystems." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "ecosystem"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/4b36a1ab-7a77-49ca-866a-e68bce2db9e5> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2022-09-19T05:09:06"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "Plant matter are plant parts that are either living or dead and contribute to the organic layer in the environment. " ;
    dcterms:modified "2024-04-17T06:15:02"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ENVO_01001121" ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/2be2d5ce-ca9d-4dff-8e79-948983944f95> ;
    skos:definition "Organic material which is primarily composed of plant structures, living or dead."@en ;
    skos:example "A collection of roots, leaves, twigs and branches on the forest floor." ;
    skos:historyNote "This concept is now deprecated from the TERN Feature Type (Release Version, 2024)." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "plant matter"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/657ee703-1122-4fdb-b0a5-7285cef27be1> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2024-04-17T06:31:08"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "Ecological dynamics refers to the complex interactions and processes that occur within ecosystems over time. These dynamics encompass a wide range of biological, physical, and chemical interactions that influence the structure, composition, and function of ecosystems. Understanding ecological dynamics is crucial for predicting changes in ecosystems, managing natural resources, and conserving biodiversity." ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-19T05:29:51+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "Garry Peterson. The Human Actor In Ecological-Economic Models. Political ecology and ecological resilience: An integration of human and ecological dynamics. Ecological Economics 35 (2000) 323 - 336" ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/237df7e1-2d92-4e9d-851e-28439bf5d8d8> ;
    skos:definition "Ecological dynamics describes coupled dynamics of human-ecological systems." ;
    skos:example "Observations made on fire ",
        "Observations may include rates of succession, turnover, productivity, decomposition, and species interactions across spatial and temporal scales. Monitoring vegetation succession and carbon fluxes following fire disturbance at a long-term ecological research (LTER) site." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "ecological dynamics"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Ecological dynamics represents process-based or system-level change within ecosystems — not a discrete object (like a plant or soil profile), but a complex feature of interest describing interacting biological, physical, and chemical processes over time. Ecological dynamics serve as a feature of interest for studies examining how ecosystems respond to natural disturbances, climatic variation, and anthropogenic pressures." ;
    schema:citation "Garry Peterson. The Human Actor In Ecological-Economic Models. Political ecology and ecological resilience: An integration of human and ecological dynamics. Ecological Economics 35 (2000) 323 - 336" .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/afc81cca-9122-4e36-823d-31dd765e9257> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2021-02-26T05:11:16"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "Geological substrates are materials and masses of earth or rock that do not show pedological development. They are not soils, but typically underlie them. The geological substrate includes the R horizon (continuous masses of rock, excluding pans, e.g. bedrock) and the part of the C horizon that shows no pedological development, but excludes the solum, buried soil horizons (including D horizons), and pans. The geological substrate beneath a soil profile may or may not be the parent material of the soil." ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-19T05:29:51+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "McDonald, R.C., Isbell, R.F., Speight, J.G., Walker, J. and Hopkins, M.S., 1998. Australian soil and land survey: field handbook (No. Ed. 2). CSIRO publishing.",
        "Valentine, P.C., 2019, Sediment classification and the characterization, identification, and mapping of geologic substrates for the glaciated Gulf of Maine seabed and other terrains, providing a physical framework for ecological research and seabed management: U.S. Geological Survey Scientific Investigations Report 2019–5073, 37 p., https://doi.org/10.3133/sir20195073." ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/8282fb22-4135-415c-8ca2-317860d102fb> ;
    skos:definition "Surface (or volume) of sediment or rock where physical, chemical, and biological processes occur, such as the movement and deposition of sediment, the formation of bedforms, and the attachment, burrowing, feeding, reproduction, and sheltering of organisms." ;
    skos:example "By origin sedimentary, igneous, metamorphic. By chemical composition alluvium, sandstone, carbonate" ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:notation "geologic-substrate" ;
    skos:prefLabel "geologic substrate"@en ;
    schema:citation "McDonald, R.C., Isbell, R.F., Speight, J.G., Walker, J. and Hopkins, M.S., 1998. Australian soil and land survey: field handbook (No. Ed. 2). CSIRO publishing.",
        "Valentine, P.C., 2019, Sediment classification and the characterization, identification, and mapping of geologic substrates for the glaciated Gulf of Maine seabed and other terrains, providing a physical framework for ecological research and seabed management: U.S. Geological Survey Scientific Investigations Report 2019–5073, 37 p., https://doi.org/10.3133/sir20195073." .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/c001183f-c6b5-4162-8de9-0c7ed0eb3bfe> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2022-05-17T03:40:34"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:modified "2025-10-14T22:18:06"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.nrs.fs.fed.us/fia/topics/dwm/glossary/> ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/a8b1a7fb-b479-48ff-b706-b3cc4691710b> ;
    skos:definition "Dead pieces of wood (diameter >10cm) including downed, dead tree and shrub boles, large limbs, and other woody pieces that are severed from their original source of growth."@en ;
    skos:example "Any observations made on coarse woody debris. For example, the count, abundance, length  and/or mass of coarse woody debris in a forest stand." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "coarse woody debris"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/c892f830-4c65-4ac2-a81a-323e2ef42657> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2022-04-20T04:18:19"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "A population of invertebrates (animals that neither possess nor develop a vertebral column) is a collection of individuals belonging to the same invertebrate species, living in the same area." ;
    dcterms:modified "2024-04-19T04:41:12"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/86758d4d-e4d9-4d03-9e5d-f528bb4a5889> ;
    skos:definition "Group of individuals of the same invertebrate species living within a given area." ;
    skos:example "The total number of male red crabs living in an area in Chrismas Island National Park." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:notation "invertebrate-population" ;
    skos:prefLabel "invertebrate population"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/d4fc54b1-0ad3-4512-86b7-d42b121ece45> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2021-03-11T00:22:45"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "Vegetation disturbance occurs at multiple temporal and spatial scales (Pickett and White, 1985). The main sources of variation in disturbance include difference in ecosystem scale, the type of disturbance and differences in the disturbance regime. Disturbance can be described in terms of their distribution, frequency, cycle/turnover time, rotation period, predictability, area, magnitude/intensity, severity and synergism with other disturbances (Pickett and White, 1985). Most disturbance produce heterogenous and patchy effects (Pickett and White, 1985)." ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-19T05:29:51+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "Pickett, S. T. A., and P. S. White. Natural disturbance and patch dynamics. (1985)." ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/7e256d28-e686-4b6a-b64a-ac1b1a8f164d> ;
    skos:closeMatch <http://vocabs.lter-europe.net/EnvThes/USLterCV_805> ;
    skos:definition "Events that disrupts the plant component of ecosystems and communities or directly impact plant population structure." ;
    skos:example "Frequency and intensity of fires or pests; the amout of land clearing; presence and abundance of cattle." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:notation "vegetation-disturbance" ;
    skos:prefLabel "vegetation disturbance"@en ;
    schema:citation "Pickett, S. T. A., and P. S. White. Natural disturbance and patch dynamics. (1985)." .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/2217ab3c-0359-42a3-9361-b3b895121841> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2024-04-18T03:18:57"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:modified "2024-04-21T22:53:57"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/a050f023-5d6a-4688-8345-d978eabe3c54> ;
    skos:definition "Vascular plants occurring at a specific region at a specific time. These plants have vascular tissue, consisting of xylem, which carries water and nutrients from the roots up the stem to the leaves, and the phloem, which carries sugar produced in the leaves to other parts of the plant." ;
    skos:example "An individual or a groups of trees sampled from a rocky area." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "vascular plant occurrence"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/57c778e4-5a82-4dfd-8087-d616ac629ab1> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2024-04-18T01:28:02"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "Any individual vascular plant, i.e., belonging to a herb, shrub, tree, palm, cycad and fern. They have vascular tissues consisting of xylem, which carries water and nutrients from the roots up the stem to the leaves, and the phloem, which carries sugar produced in the leaves to other parts of the plant." ;
    dcterms:modified "2024-04-21T22:53:42"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/a050f023-5d6a-4688-8345-d978eabe3c54> ;
    skos:definition "A vascular plant individual is a plant individual with vascular tissue." ;
    skos:example "A tree or a shrub species, tagged and identified to its species level and studied for its diameter growth over multiple site visits." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "vascular plant individual"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/7e256d28-e686-4b6a-b64a-ac1b1a8f164d> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2021-03-09T07:21:46"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "In ecology, a disturbance is a temporary change in average environmental conditions that causes a pronounced change in an ecosystem. Outside disturbance forces often act quickly and with great effect, sometimes resulting in the removal of large amounts of biomass. Ecological disturbances include fires, flooding, windstorm, insect outbreaks, as well as anthropogenic disturbances such as forest clearing and the introduction of exotic species." ;
    dcterms:modified "2024-06-28T02:56:45"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "https://vocab.lternet.edu/vocab/vocab/index.php?tema=662&/disturbance",
        "https://w3id.org/inbio#_000001" ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/657ee703-1122-4fdb-b0a5-7285cef27be1> ;
    skos:closeMatch <http://vocabs.lter-europe.net/EnvThes/20373> ;
    skos:definition "Events that disrupts ecosystem, community, or population structure and changes resources, substrate availability, or the physical environment." ;
    skos:example "1. A fire event that causes a change in the community profile in the Litchfield National Park, NT; 2. Tropical cyclones leading to disturbance in a forest community structure." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:notation "land-surface-disturbance" ;
    skos:prefLabel "disturbance"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/8cadf069-01d7-4420-b454-cae37740c2a2> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2021-05-06T05:15:16"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-02-24T22:38:22"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/5bf7ae21-a454-440b-bdd7-f2fe982d8de4> ;
    skos:definition "Land area selected from within a survey region which abiotic and biotic properties are sampled." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "plot"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/95e72362-8575-46e9-bc60-a45f48b95390> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2024-04-18T03:48:07"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "These are plants such as trees, shrubs, palms, cycads etc. which have true vascular tubes/tissues to transport food and water." ;
    dcterms:modified "2024-04-21T22:54:10"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/a050f023-5d6a-4688-8345-d978eabe3c54> ;
    skos:definition "The vascular plant population refers to the total count of individuals of a particular species of vascular plants." ;
    skos:example "1. A count of the total number of Eucalyptus citriodora.; 2. The total number of seedlings and adult pine trees (Pinus radiata) in a forest stand." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "vascular plant population"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/b64cfbb3-86a2-43b3-a749-11243805cb85> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2024-04-17T23:54:43"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "These plants have vascular tissue, consisting of xylem, which carries water and nutrients from the roots up the stem to the leaves, and the phloem, which carries sugar produced in the leaves to other parts of the plant." ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-19T05:29:51+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/a050f023-5d6a-4688-8345-d978eabe3c54> ;
    skos:definition "A community of plants such as the herbs, shrubs, trees, palms, cycads and ferns." ;
    skos:exactMatch <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCIT_C14336> ;
    skos:example "A community of trees, shrubs and ferns sampled from a protected area." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "vascular plants community"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/db6d3a22-8bf4-4e27-a9a4-a49e228b6f52> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2024-04-16T04:45:42"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "A gaseous envelope gravitationally bound to a celestial body (e.g., a planet, its satellite, or a star)." ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-19T05:29:51+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "Glossary of the American Meteorological Society, 2018. http://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/Atmosphere" ;
    skos:definition "An atmosphere is a layer of gases surrounding a material body of sufficient mass that is held in place by the gravity of the body."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ENVO_01000267> ;
    skos:example "Atmospheric CO₂ concentration measured at 2 m above ground level at Warra Tall Eucalypt, Tasmania, Australia." ;
    skos:historyNote "Added as a top concept for the TERN Feature Type (Release version, 2024)." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "atmosphere"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    schema:citation "Glossary of the American Meteorological Society, 2018. http://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/Atmosphere" .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/e6ed6e58-5916-4d31-9ed5-109ab3436fce> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2021-04-20T03:43:43"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "Dead plant material, such as leaves, bark, needles, twigs, and other woody debris that has fallen to the ground. Plant litter make up a large portion of above ground net primary production of all terrestrial ecosystems." ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-19T05:29:51+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "Chapin III, F. Stuart, Pamela A. Matson, and Peter Vitousek. Principles of terrestrial ecosystem ecology. Springer Science & Business Media, 2011." ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/2be2d5ce-ca9d-4dff-8e79-948983944f95> ;
    skos:closeMatch <http://vocabs.lter-europe.net/EnvThes/20955> ;
    skos:definition "Partly decomposed plant residues (i.e. wood, leaf or root) and related characteristics on the surface of soil." ;
    skos:example "Leaf litterfall collected from forest floor can include observations on 'litter depth' and 'litter mass'. Such observations qualify under 'plant litter' as a feature of interest." ;
    skos:historyNote "Added as a narrower concept of 'plant' in the TERN Feature Type (Release version, 2024)." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:notation "plant-litter" ;
    skos:prefLabel "plant litter"@en ;
    schema:citation "Chapin III, F. Stuart, Pamela A. Matson, and Peter Vitousek. Principles of terrestrial ecosystem ecology. Springer Science & Business Media, 2011." .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/237df7e1-2d92-4e9d-851e-28439bf5d8d8> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2024-04-17T06:26:26"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:modified "2025-10-13T22:56:25"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "https://nationalgeographic.org"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    skos:definition "The biosphere is made up of the parts of Earth where life exists. The biosphere extends from the deepest root systems of trees, to the dark environment of ocean trenches, to lush rain forests and high mountaintops."@en ;
    skos:example "Biosphere as a feature of interest can be modelled to any earth observations (e.g., atmospheric CO2 concentrations, biomass, etc.) made on entities (either terrestrial or aquatic) at a planetary scale where life exists." ;
    skos:historyNote "Added as a top concept for the TERN Feature Type (Release version, 2024)." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "biosphere"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "In the W3C/OGC SSN and SOSA frameworks, the biosphere represents a feature of interest at a planetary scale. It is often the subject of environmental observations, modelling, and sustainability assessments, encompassing terrestrial, aquatic, and atmospheric life systems." ;
    skos:topConceptOf <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/3d355008-117e-48ec-a822-adb09495ac78> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2024-04-16T05:03:57"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "Organisms are classified based on how similar they are. Historically, similarity was determined by examining the physical characteristics of an organism but modern classification uses a variety of techniques including genetic analysis. Organisms are classified according to a system of seven taxonomic ranks: Kingdom; Phylum; Class; Order; Family; Genus; Species"@en ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-19T05:29:51+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "Global Change Master Directory (GCMD). 2024. GCMD Keywords, Version 18.1. Greenbelt, MD: Earth Science Data and Information System, Earth Science Projects Division, Goddard Space Flight Center (GSFC) National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA). URL (GCMD Keyword Forum Page): https://forum.earthdata.nasa.gov/app.php/tag/GCMD%20Keywords" ;
    skos:altLabel "systematics"@en,
        "taxonomic classification"@en,
        "taxonomy"@en ;
    skos:definition "Biological classification is the process of classification of living organisms into groups based on shared common traits and genetic information."@en ;
    skos:example "Example of biological classification as a feature of interest include observations made on a 'taxon' (e.g., Genus: Eucalyptus) from which their taxonomic ranks such as domain, kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, and species (if identified) can be organized." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "biological classification"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    schema:citation "Global Change Master Directory (GCMD). 2024. GCMD Keywords, Version 18.1. Greenbelt, MD: Earth Science Data and Information System, Earth Science Projects Division, Goddard Space Flight Center (GSFC) National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA). URL (GCMD Keyword Forum Page): https://forum.earthdata.nasa.gov/app.php/tag/GCMD%20Keywords" .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/df7fd606-8145-4206-b66e-b86444888802> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2021-04-20T04:15:03"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "Organic matter provides the fuel for fire. The diversity of types of natural fire is, in part, a result of the corresponding diversity in vegetation and litter (Cornnwell et al., 2015). The fire regime in a place will be dependent on the vegetation fule properties, along wiht climate, weather and ignition sources (Johnson, 1992; Swetnam, 1993). Vegation fuels can be classified into three major categories: crown, surface and ground." ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-19T05:29:51+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "Cornwell, William K., et al. Flammability across the gymnosperm phylogeny: the importance of litter particle size. New Phytologist 206.2 (2015): 672-681.",
        "Cruz, M.G., Gould, J.S., Hollis, J.J. and McCaw, W., 2018. A Hierarchical classification of wildland fire fuels for Australian vegetation types. Fire, 1(1), p.13.",
        "Johnson, Kirk R. Leaf-fossil evidence for extensive floral extinction at the Cretaceous-Tertiary boundary, North Dakota, USA. Cretaceous Research 13.1 (1992): 91-117.",
        "Swetnam, Thomas W. Fire history and climate change in giant sequoia groves. Science 262.5135 (1993): 885-889." ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/2be2d5ce-ca9d-4dff-8e79-948983944f95> ;
    skos:definition "Dead and alive vegetation characteristics relevant to its flammability." ;
    skos:example "Canopy length and load; bark fuels; biomass of dead and live plants on the ground." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:notation "vegetation-fuel" ;
    skos:prefLabel "vegetation fuel"@en ;
    schema:citation "Cornwell, William K., et al. Flammability across the gymnosperm phylogeny: the importance of litter particle size. New Phytologist 206.2 (2015): 672-681.",
        "Cruz, M.G., Gould, J.S., Hollis, J.J. and McCaw, W., 2018. A Hierarchical classification of wildland fire fuels for Australian vegetation types. Fire, 1(1), p.13.",
        "Johnson, Kirk R. Leaf-fossil evidence for extensive floral extinction at the Cretaceous-Tertiary boundary, North Dakota, USA. Cretaceous Research 13.1 (1992): 91-117.",
        "Swetnam, Thomas W. Fire history and climate change in giant sequoia groves. Science 262.5135 (1993): 885-889." .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/5c93aeef-c625-4117-85be-3a886416f2be> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2024-04-16T06:03:07"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "A population of vertebrates (animals that possess or develop a vertebral column) is a collection of individuals belonging to the same vertebrate species, living in the same area." ;
    dcterms:modified "2024-04-19T04:44:51"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/c242f606-1df2-4222-a5f1-a899ae66fde4> ;
    skos:definition "Group of individuals of the same vertebrate species living within a given area." ;
    skos:example "The total count of Blue-faced Parrotfinch (Erythrura trichroa) from a tropical rainforest.; The number of male Koalas (Phascolarctos cinereus) in a Protected Area." ;
    skos:historyNote "Added to the TERN Feature Type (Release version, 2024)." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "vertebrate population"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/8282fb22-4135-415c-8ca2-317860d102fb> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2021-02-25T01:11:44"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "Most of the attributes for land surface deal with its physical properties (e.g. aspect, elevation). Some other reflect properties within (e.g. microrelief). Some attributes reflect the influence of present or past land use practice (e.g. disturbance of site, erosion)." ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-19T05:29:51+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "McDonald, R.C., Isbell, R.F., Speight, J.G., Walker, J. and Hopkins, M.S., 1998. Australian soil and land survey: field handbook (No. Ed. 2). CSIRO publishing." ;
    skos:definition "Surface layer of Earth which is primarily composed of solid material and is not covered by oceans or other bodies of water." ;
    skos:exactMatch <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ENVO_01001785> ;
    skos:example "1. The average slope of the mountain range; 2. The dominant lithology of the Great Artesian Basin." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:notation "land-surface" ;
    skos:prefLabel "land surface"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    schema:citation "McDonald, R.C., Isbell, R.F., Speight, J.G., Walker, J. and Hopkins, M.S., 1998. Australian soil and land survey: field handbook (No. Ed. 2). CSIRO publishing." .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/86758d4d-e4d9-4d03-9e5d-f528bb4a5889> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2024-04-19T04:37:35"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "An invertebrate is any animal that lacks a vertebral column, commonly known as a spine or backbone. Unlike the cartilaginous or bony vertebrates, invertebrates make up over 90 percent of all living animal species. They span a diverse range, including sea stars, earthworms, sponges, insects, and jellyfish." ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-19T05:29:51+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "Gould, J. and W.Keaton. 1996. Biological Science. W.W. Norton and Company. New York." ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/f6a6cdf8-d8cf-4e2d-b2c1-febd8a41a07b> ;
    skos:definition "Invertebrates are animals lacking a backbone, or without bones." ;
    skos:example "Any observation made on the animal class 'invertebrates' (e.g., ants, beetles, moths, etc.) from a study area." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "invertebrates"@en ;
    schema:citation "Gould, J. and W.Keaton. 1996. Biological Science. W.W. Norton and Company. New York." .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/a050f023-5d6a-4688-8345-d978eabe3c54> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2024-04-21T22:52:42"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-19T05:29:51+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/881bcb98-1c31-4710-a9a4-58dbe1bc4a95> ;
    skos:definition "The flowering herbs, shrubs, trees and ferns. These plants have vascular tissue, consisting of xylem, which carries water and nutrients from the roots up the stem to the leaves, and the phloem, which carries sugar produced in the leaves to other parts of the plant." ;
    skos:exactMatch <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCIT_C14336> ;
    skos:example "Observations made on vascular plants can include ecological, physiological and or genetic features. For instance, measurement of leaf carbon content on all vacular plants within a forest plot." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "vascular plants"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/a8b1a7fb-b479-48ff-b706-b3cc4691710b> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:description "Dead wood refers to the dead woody components of a tree, either free standing, leaning or lying on the ground, which contributes to biomass and ecological processes in natural ecosystems." ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-19T05:29:51+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "Rondeux, J., Sanchez, C. Review of indicators and field methods for monitoring biodiversity within national forest inventories. Core variable: Deadwood. Environ Monit Assess 164, 617–630 (2010). https://doi.org/10.1007/s10661-009-0917-6" ;
    skos:altLabel "dead woody material" ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/2be2d5ce-ca9d-4dff-8e79-948983944f95> ;
    skos:definition "All non-living woody detritus comprising stems, twigs and roots but excluding the deadwood parts of living trees."@en ;
    skos:example "A coarse woody debris lying on a forest floor; a free-standing or leaning tree, without any living parts." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:notation "dead-wood" ;
    skos:prefLabel "dead wood"@en ;
    schema:citation "Rondeux, J., Sanchez, C. Review of indicators and field methods for monitoring biodiversity within national forest inventories. Core variable: Deadwood. Environ Monit Assess 164, 617–630 (2010). https://doi.org/10.1007/s10661-009-0917-6" .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/c242f606-1df2-4222-a5f1-a899ae66fde4> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2024-04-19T04:37:51"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "A vertebrate is an animal with a backbone, scientifically classified under the subphylum Vertebrata. These animals are characterized by several key features that include: Backbone; Muscular System; Central Nervous System; Distinct Head; Pharyngeal Slits and Gills." ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-19T05:29:51+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "Starr, C. and R.Taggart. 1998. Biology: the unity and diversity of life. Wadsworth Publishing Company. Belmont, California." ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/f6a6cdf8-d8cf-4e2d-b2c1-febd8a41a07b> ;
    skos:definition "Vertebrates are chordates with a backbone, either of cartilage or bone, and a brain located inside a protective chamber of skull bones." ;
    skos:example "Any observations (morphological, physiological, genetic, etc.) made on the animal class 'vertebrates' (e.g., mammals, birds, etc.) in a study area." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "vertebrates"@en ;
    schema:citation "Starr, C. and R.Taggart. 1998. Biology: the unity and diversity of life. Wadsworth Publishing Company. Belmont, California." .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/ea6c4b26-2fa1-4d0e-9413-b53f248f2269> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2024-04-21T22:53:01"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-19T05:29:51+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/881bcb98-1c31-4710-a9a4-58dbe1bc4a95> ;
    skos:definition "A plant that does not have vascular tubes/tissues to transport food and water. It belongs to the division Bryophyta, which includes liverwort, and moss." ;
    skos:exactMatch <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCIT_C14340> ;
    skos:example "Observations made on non-vascular plants can include ecological, physiological and or genetic features. For instance, observations on the total abundance of all non-vascular plants (e.g., bryophytes and lichens) within a forest plot." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "non-vascular plants"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/1554c27a-e777-49da-abc3-10296734f80b> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2024-04-16T05:24:48"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "A single physiologically distinct vertebrate individual (animals that possess a vertebral column), which are tagged and studied." ;
    dcterms:modified "2024-04-19T04:44:22"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/c242f606-1df2-4222-a5f1-a899ae66fde4> ;
    skos:definition "A vertebrate which is physiologically distinct and independent." ;
    skos:example "A collection of individually tagged vertebrates (such as birds, mammals, reptiles etc.) that are monitored for their abundance over time." ;
    skos:historyNote "Added to the TERN Feature Type (Release version, 2024)." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "vertebrate individual"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/5cd1dbb0-c30e-4c8e-ae1b-0b388638163c> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2024-04-16T05:48:56"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "A vertebrate occurrence (animals that neither possess or develop a vertebral column) is a sample of a vertebrate individual or group at a particular point and time." ;
    dcterms:modified "2024-04-19T04:44:40"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/c242f606-1df2-4222-a5f1-a899ae66fde4> ;
    skos:definition "An existence of a single vertebrate or group of vertebrates sampled at a particular place at a particular time." ;
    skos:example "The presence of vertebrates (e.g., Koalas, Gliders and Lorikeets) in a tree canopy." ;
    skos:historyNote "Updated the heirarchy of the TERN Feature Type (Release version, 2024)." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "vertebrate occurrence"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/98e8d72d-f361-41ed-af9d-6e7f90c1dfce> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2021-03-16T05:07:09"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "It's a natural body consisting of layers (soil horizons) that are primarily composed of minerals, mixed with at least some organic matter, which differ from their parent materials in their texture, structure, consistency, color, chemical, biological and other characteristics. It is the loose covering of fine rock particles that covers the surface of the earth." ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-19T05:29:51+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "Simons, B., Wilson, P., Ritchie, A. and Cox, S., 2013, April. ANZSoilML: an Australian-New Zealand standard for exchange of soil data. In EGU General Assembly Conference Abstracts (pp. EGU2013-6802).",
        "http://cerrado.linkeddata.es/ecology/ccon#Soil" ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/8282fb22-4135-415c-8ca2-317860d102fb> ;
    skos:definition "Natural body comprised of minerals, soil organic matter, living organisms, gas, and water." ;
    skos:exactMatch <http://vocabs.lter-europe.net/EnvThes/24> ;
    skos:example "1) The texture of a soil; 2) The water holding capacity of a soil." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:notation "soil" ;
    skos:prefLabel "soil"@en ;
    schema:citation "Simons, B., Wilson, P., Ritchie, A. and Cox, S., 2013, April. ANZSoilML: an Australian-New Zealand standard for exchange of soil data. In EGU General Assembly Conference Abstracts (pp. EGU2013-6802)." .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/05dac53a-269c-4699-9673-bf99a9406b14> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2022-03-01T01:52:56"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "An administrative area is a human-defined region for business purposes. These areas are typically defined by defined boundaries and serve governmental and administrative purposes and include areas reserved for nature preservation; areas allocated for mineral exploitation; or zoned for suburbs."@en ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-10-19T05:29:51+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "Administrative area. (n.d.) WordNet 3.0, Farlex clipart collection. (2003-2008). Retrieved June 22 2023 from https://www.thefreedictionary.com/Administrative+area"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "adminstrative region" ;
    skos:broadMatch <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ENVO_00000004> ;
    skos:definition "A region of the earth's surface, terrestrial or marine or mixed, that is defined for human purposes and is designated with a specific level of administrative authority."@en ;
    skos:example "Some examples of an 'administrative area' are: 1) Kakadu National Park; 2) Tom Price Mine; 3) Southern Kangaroo Island Marine Park; 4) Shorncliffe (suburb of Brisbane); 5) State Jurisdiction of New South Wales; 6) Meshblock 11234 (ABS census region)"@en ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:notation "administrative-area" ;
    skos:prefLabel "administrative area"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    schema:citation "Administrative area. (n.d.) WordNet 3.0, Farlex clipart collection. (2003-2008). Retrieved June 22 2023 from https://www.thefreedictionary.com/Administrative+area"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/881bcb98-1c31-4710-a9a4-58dbe1bc4a95> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2024-04-17T05:59:57"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:modified "2025-10-14T03:14:04"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "http://www.fao.org/aims/aos/cwr.owl#Plant"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/3d355008-117e-48ec-a822-adb09495ac78> ;
    skos:definition "Any of a kingdom (Plantae) of living beings typically lacking locomotive movement or obvious sensory organs, generally making its own food, possessing cell walls, and unlimited growth." ;
    skos:example "Observations made on plant can include ecological, physiological and or genetic features. For instance, measurement of leaf chlorophyll content on all plants within a forest plot." ;
    skos:historyNote "Added as a narrower concept of 'biological classification' in the TERN Feature Type (Release version, 2024)." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "plant"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Plants serve as a feature of interest for ecological, physiological, and genetic observations including morphology, biomass, productivity, phenology, and nutrient dynamics. Observations may be made at individual, population, or community levels."@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/2be2d5ce-ca9d-4dff-8e79-948983944f95> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2021-03-11T00:21:46"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "Vegetation is an assemblage of plant species occurring in a given geographic area. They often comprise of layers/stratum that is characteristic of the vegetation type." ;
    dcterms:modified "2024-04-17T06:29:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "http://www.fao.org/aims/aos/cwr.owl#Vegetation" ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/237df7e1-2d92-4e9d-851e-28439bf5d8d8> ;
    skos:definition "The assemblage of plant species in a given area; also used as a general term for plant life." ;
    skos:exactMatch <http://vocabs.lter-europe.net/EnvThes/20535> ;
    skos:example "1. A eucalypt woodland; 2. A Mallee shrubland; 3. A simple notophyll vine forest." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:notation "vegetation" ;
    skos:prefLabel "vegetation"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/5bf7ae21-a454-440b-bdd7-f2fe982d8de4> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-02-24T22:33:39"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:closeMatch <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCIT_C80403> ;
    skos:definition "A place in which study/protocol/sampling activities are conducted." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:prefLabel "site"@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/f6a6cdf8-d8cf-4e2d-b2c1-febd8a41a07b> a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:created "2023-12-12T03:42:09"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "Animals are organisms with eukaryotic cells that derive nutrition from external sources (of organic nature) and are capable of voluntary movement in search of food and shelter "@en ;
    dcterms:modified "2025-10-01T06:14:54"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:source "http://ncicb.nci.nih.gov/xml/owl/EVS/Thesaurus.owl#C14182"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    skos:broader <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/3d355008-117e-48ec-a822-adb09495ac78> ;
    skos:definition "A living organism (non-human) that has membranous cell walls, requires oxygen and organic foods, and is capable of voluntary movement, as distinguished from a plant or mineral."@en ;
    skos:example "Any animal which includes vertebrates such as amphibians, birds,  mammals, or invertebrates such as ants, beetles, etc."@en ;
    skos:historyNote "Updated the heirarchy for the TERN Feature Type (Release version, 2024)." ;
    skos:inScheme <http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> ;
    skos:notation "animal"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "animal"@en .

<http://linked.data.gov.au/def/tern-cv/68af3d25-c801-4089-afff-cf701e2bd61d> a skos:ConceptScheme ;
    dcterms:created "2021-02-01T04:23:43"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:description "<p><div><p>A <em>feature type</em> is a conceptual category of <strong>feature of interest</strong> used within the TERN Ontology to support data integration, harmonisation, and interoperability across ecological datasets. Feature types provide a common semantic framework through which observations and measured properties from different monitoring programs and surveys can be linked and interpreted consistently.<p>For example, the feature type <strong>Plant Occurrence</strong> represents the presence of a plant within a sampling unit (such as a plot, quadrat, or transect). Observable properties associated with a plant occurrence may include <em>species name</em>, <em>plant height</em>, <em>stem diameter</em>, <em>canopy cover</em>, and other attributes recorded during ecological surveys. By assigning these observations to the feature type <em>Plant Occurrence</em>, data collected using different survey protocols can be harmonised around a common ecological concept: the occurrence of a plant at a particular place and time.<p>In contrast, the feature type <strong>Plant Individual</strong> refers to a specific, identifiable plant that can be repeatedly observed through time. Observable properties may be similar to those recorded for a plant occurrence, including <em>species name</em>, <em>plant height</em>, and <em>stem diameter</em>. However, the distinguishing characteristic of a plant individual is the existence of a persistent identifier, such as a <em>stem ID</em>, <em>tree tag number</em>, or another unique identifier. This allows observations collected across multiple census events or monitoring campaigns to be unambiguously linked to the same biological entity over time. Consequently, a plant individual supports longitudinal analyses such as growth, mortality, recruitment, and condition change.<p>The distinction between these two feature types is therefore important:<ul><li><strong>Plant Occurrence</strong> focuses on the observation of a plant's presence within a survey context, without necessarily identifying the same plant through time.<li><strong>Plant Individual</strong> focuses on a uniquely identified plant that can be tracked and re-observed across multiple monitoring events.</ul><p>Feature types therefore act as harmonisation points within the ontology, enabling observations from different ecological surveys and monitoring programs to be integrated while preserving their ecological meaning and context.</div><p> "^^rdf:HTML ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-08-04T01:56:22"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:definition "The scheme feature type is an observation feature on which parameters or observable properties are derived." ;
    skos:prefLabel "Feature Type"@en .
